    "summary": "DMT produces its effects through specific interactions with brain chemistry and neural circuitry.",
    "markdown": "## Classification\n- **Type**: Scientific Framework\n- **Domain**: Neurobiology\n\n## Core Concept\nDMT produces its effects through specific interactions with brain chemistry and neural circuitry.\n\n## Pharmacology\n- **Receptor**: Primarily 5-HT2A serotonin receptors\n- **Mechanism**: Agonist—activates receptor\n- **Location**: Dense in cortex, especially visual areas\n- **Duration**: 5-20 minutes (short due to rapid metabolism)\n\n## Brain Regions Affected\n- **Visual cortex**: Hyperactivation produces geometric visuals\n- **Default mode network (DMN)**: Deactivation allows ego dissolution\n- **Thalamus**: Disruption of sensory gating\n- **Pineal gland**: Historical/metaphysical interest\n\n## Neural Effects\n- **Synaptic plasticity**: Temporary enhancement\n- **Neurotransmitter release**: Serotonin, dopamine, others\n- **Network connectivity**: Altered functional connectivity\n- **Entropy**: Increased neural signal diversity\n\n## Consciousness Effects\n- **Ego dissolution**: DMN deactivation\n- **Visual hallucinations**: Visual cortex hyperactivation\n- **Time distortion**: Disruption of time-keeping circuits\n- **Entity encounters**: Unknown—possibly temporal lobe activation\n\n## Therapeutic Potential\n- **Depression**: DMN reset, new perspectives\n- **PTSD**: Fear extinction, memory reprocessing\n- **Addiction**: Craving reduction, insight\n- **End-of-life anxiety**: Death acceptance, transcendence\n\n## Research Status\n- **Legal barriers**: Schedule I status limits research\n- **Recent studies**: Johns Hopkins, Imperial College leading\n- **Findings**: Safety in controlled settings, therapeutic potential\n\n## Open Questions\n- **Why entities?**: Neural basis of entity encounters unknown\n- **Why similar reports?**: Common neural architecture or shared reality?\n- **Long-term effects**: Integration, personality changes",
