    "shortTitle": "The Dissociative Path",
    "summary": "The Dissociative Path is the spiritual approach of detachment, observation, and non-identification—using dissociation as a tool for liberation rather than escape.",
    "markdown": "## Classification\n- **Type**: Spiritual/Cosmological Concept\n- **Domain**: Non-Dual Philosophy\n\n## Core Concept\nThe Dissociative Path is the spiritual approach of detachment, observation, and non-identification—using dissociation as a tool for liberation rather than escape.\n\n## Characteristics\n- **Detachment**: Not caught in drama\n- **Observation**: Witness consciousness\n- **Non-identification**: Not the body, not the mind\n- **Emptiness**: Void as full of potential\n- **Play**: Reality as game\n\n## DXM Connection\n- **Dissociation**: Separation from ego\n- **Observation**: Watching from outside\n- **The Void**: Empty yet full\n- **Play**: Reality as cosmic joke\n- **Liberation**: Freedom through detachment\n\n## Spiritual Traditions\n- **Advaita Vedanta**: Non-dual awareness\n- **Buddhism**: Non-attachment, emptiness\n- **Taoism**: Wu wei, effortless action\n- **Stoicism**: Equanimity, acceptance\n\n## The Path\n1. **Recognition**: See the constructed nature of self\n2. **Detachment**: Step back from identification\n3. **Observation**: Witness without judgment\n4. **Acceptance**: Allow what is\n5. **Liberation**: Freedom from suffering\n\n## Dangers\n- **Bypass**: Using detachment to avoid\n- **Nihilism**: Believing nothing matters\n- **Disconnection**: Losing human connection\n- **Addiction**: Escaping rather than growing\n\n## Integration\n- **Balance**: Detachment with engagement\n- **Compassion**: Care without attachment\n- **Action**: Do without being attached to results\n- **Play**: Take life seriously but not too seriously\n\n## Cosmological Position\n- **Function**: Liberation through detachment\n- **Purpose**: Freedom from suffering\n- **Method**: Observation, non-identification\n- **Integration**: Balance with engagement",
    "sourcePath": "wiki/DXM/Cosmology/The_Dissociative_Path.md",
    "safetyRedacted": false
